<CDName>giac_procedures1</CDName>
<CDBase>https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd</CDBase>
<CDURL>https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd/giac_procedures1.ocd</CDURL>
<CDReviewDate>2027-11-28</CDReviewDate>
<CDStatus>private</CDStatus>
<CDDate>2026-11-28</CDDate>
<CDVersion>0</CDVersion>
<CDRevision>1</CDRevision>

<Description>
Requests to the GIAC computer algebra system (https://www-fourier.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/~parisse/giac.html),
to be made with scscp1.procedure_call, as an SCSCP service's procedures are.
The symbols of this CD name requests, not mathematical objects: what GIAC
answers depends on its version and settings. They belong in a procedure_call
only; a result, a claim or evidence is written in official symbols, with its
relation to the request explicit (equal under the premises, the solutions of,
an approximation of). Readers refuse these symbols elsewhere, and refuse this
CD unless their caller enables it.
</Description>

<CDDefinition>
<Name>simplify</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's simplify command, simplify(e): GIAC returns a form of e GIAC considers simpler; its value equals e under the premises of the call.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. The arguments (e) are
OpenMath objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the
call are given by the attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA>
      <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="simplify"/>
      <OMV name="x"/>
    </OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>expand</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's expand command, expand(e): GIAC returns e with products and powers expanded; its value equals e.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. The arguments (e) are
OpenMath objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the
call are given by the attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA>
      <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="expand"/>
      <OMV name="x"/>
    </OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>factor</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's factor command, factor(e): GIAC returns e factored (over the rationals, or over the field the premises allow); its value equals e.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. The arguments (e) are
OpenMath objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the
call are given by the attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA>
      <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="factor"/>
      <OMV name="x"/>
    </OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>solve</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's solve command, solve(r, v): GIAC returns the solutions in the variable v of the relation r (or of a list of relations), as GIAC returns them: a list of values, or of relations for several variables.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. The arguments (r, v) are
OpenMath objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the
call are given by the attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA>
      <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="solve"/>
      <OMV name="x"/>
    </OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>evalf</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's evalf command, evalf(e) or evalf(e, d): GIAC returns a floating-point approximation of e, with d significant digits if given; an approximation, never an equality.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. The arguments (e, d) are
OpenMath objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the
call are given by the attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA>
      <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="evalf"/>
      <OMV name="x"/>
    </OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>series</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's series command, series(e, v, a, n): GIAC returns the series expansion of e in v at a, to order n, as GIAC returns it (with its order term).
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. The arguments (e, v, a, n) are
OpenMath objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the
call are given by the attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA>
      <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="series"/>
      <OMV name="x"/>
    </OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>desolve</Name>
<Role>application</Role>
<Description>
A request to GIAC's desolve command, desolve(r, y): GIAC returns the solutions of the differential equation r for the unknown function y, as GIAC returns them.
This symbol names a request, not a mathematical function: the answer depends on
the GIAC version and the settings of the service. It may appear only as the
head of the argument of scscp1.procedure_call, never in a result, a claim or
evidence; a reader refuses it anywhere else. The arguments (r, y) are
OpenMath objects in official symbols. The premises and the complex mode of the
call are given by the attributions giac_procedures1.premises and
giac_procedures1.complex_mode on that argument.
</Description>
<Example>
<OMOBJ xmlns="http://www.openmath.org/OpenMath" version="2.0" cdbase="http://www.openmath.org/cd">
  <OMA>
    <OMS cd="scscp1" name="procedure_call"/>
    <OMA>
      <OMS cdbase="https://juliagiac.github.io/giac-openmath/cd" cd="giac_procedures1" name="desolve"/>
      <OMV name="x"/>
    </OMA>
  </OMA>
</OMOBJ>
</Example>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>premises</Name>
<Role>attribution</Role>
<Description>
The assumptions GIAC makes during the call (GIAC's assume), as a boolean OpenMath object in official symbols: a relation, or logic1.and of relations, about the variables of the call (for example relation1.gt(x, 0)). The answer of the call holds under them.
</Description>
</CDDefinition>
<CDDefinition>
<Name>complex_mode</Name>
<Role>attribution</Role>
<Description>
Whether GIAC computes over the complex numbers during the call (GIAC's complex_mode): logic1.true or logic1.false. Without it, the service's setting applies, and the answer should say which.
</Description>
</CDDefinition>
</CD>
